Then we romanticize it.

Not falsely. Not by painting gold over rot. By noticing the charged edge of things—the steam off coffee in a cold kitchen, headlights on wet asphalt, your kid’s hand disappearing into yours, the strange dignity of doing ordinary things well.

A romanticized life is not pretending everything is beautiful. It is treating attention as an instrument that can reveal beauty, meaning, tension, comedy, danger, tenderness, and myth already present in the scene.

We keep reality. We turn up the saturation.

A workout becomes a proving ground.
A drive becomes a procession through weather and light.
Dinner becomes a small household ritual.
A hard season becomes a chapter, not the whole book.
Work becomes craft.
Parenthood becomes inheritance in motion.
Marriage becomes two people repeatedly choosing the same fire.
A mistake becomes scar tissue with information inside it.

And importantly: romanticization should increase contact with life, not replace it. If the story makes you notice more, care more, act more deliberately, it is serving you. If it makes you ignore what is actually happening, the lens has become a veil.

So yes.

Romanticized-life mode: reality intact, meaning amplified.